#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Read what a model would call a run of calls the author left unnamed.

`_fold_phases` folds a run of external calls into the name the author wrote in
a comment above it. Measured over five codebases that covers a minority of the
opportunities — 16 of 118 runs in sqlmap, 32 of 73 in nltk — because most runs
have no comment. Naming those is model work, and so is the judgement that goes
with it: whether a run is machinery worth folding or the function's actual work,
which no structural test got right. Package diversity was tried and separates a
six-call setup run from a five-call dicom run, then fails on a nine-call dicom
run with `os` and `numpy` mixed in.

This script exists because that has to be read before it is built. The rendered
map is the wrong instrument for judging a model — `./corpus` unsets
`RECCE_MODEL` precisely because output that changes between runs cannot be
diffed — so the arrangement is the one CLAUDE.md already describes for
comparing models: ask directly over a fixed candidate set and read the answers.

Two modes, and calibrate is the one to run first.

`--calibrate` asks about the runs that DO carry a comment and prints the
model's answer beside the author's. Those are ground truth: if the model cannot
land near a phrase the author wrote, it has no business naming the ones the
author didn't.

The default mode asks about the unnamed runs, which is the actual work, and
counts how often the model declines. A model that never declines is not
exercising judgement, it is generating labels, and the fold would then bury the
substance along with the machinery.

What the full run over sqlmap and nltk established, 111 unnamed runs:

- Naming works. 108 of 111 answered, in about two minutes on the 96GB box.
- Judgement does not. It declined 3, which is 2.7%, on a set where a good
  fraction should have been refused. Asking a model to decline does not work,
  and no rewording of the NONE clause is likely to fix a 97% answer rate.
- `coverage` is a dead signal and this is why it is still printed: median 6%,
  maximum 63%, one run above 50%. A run of external calls is inherently a small
  slice of its function, so the measure that was meant to separate "this run IS
  the function" cannot, because no run is. That hypothesis was wrong.
- `restatement` is live. It flags 7 of 108, of which two or three are genuinely
  misleading -- `parseSqliteTableSchema` named `parse sqlite table schema`,
  `createGithubIssue` named `create github issue` at 6% coverage, claiming the
  whole job for a fifteenth of the code. The rest are qualified enough to
  stand: `set up edit field`, `create results box widgets`.

Two ways of asking for that judgement were then built and measured, because
both read as obviously right. Neither works, and the table is the reason
`--full` and `--two-stage` still exist: to keep the refutation runnable.

                                    declines on   declines on
                                    unnamed runs  author-named runs   gap
    run's own lines, one question         2.7%          0%           -2.7
    whole function, one question           15%         18%           +3
    whole function, two questions          61%         77%          +16
    rendered map, two questions            94%         95%           +1

The right-hand column is ground truth inverted. Those are runs the author
thought worth a heading, so a gate that judges should decline them *less* often
than the unnamed ones, and the gap should be negative and large.

It never is. The decline rate climbs from 3% to 94% as the prompt gets more
context and a more explicit invitation to refuse, and the gap stays inside
noise the whole way. The model is answering the framing of the question, not
the content of the run.

A fifth was run because all four had `think: false`, copied from `notes._ask`
where it exists for a budget reason -- reasoning shares `num_predict` with the
answer, and at 40 tokens it eats all of it. That is a truncation problem, not a
verdict on reasoning, so the table above tested a model with its reasoning
switched off and said so nowhere.

Turned on, with the ceiling lifted to 2000, it splits by question. Naming does
not converge: 7385 characters of reasoning at 2000 tokens and 28473 at 8000,
`done_reason` still `length` and the answer still empty both times. The closed
KEEP-or-DROP question stops on its own in three to ten seconds.

So thinking was given to the second question only, which is the half that was
failing anyway, and it made the gap worse:

    two questions, no thinking     61% DROP unnamed   77% author-named   +16
    two questions, thinking        53% DROP unnamed   86% author-named   +33

with 18% of asks unreadable, the reasoning having run to the ceiling. The
author-named side is 14 verdicts after the paragraph bound shrank that set, so
it is a small sample and suggestive rather than decisive -- but the direction
is the same one every other configuration showed.

The fourth row was run because the third's failure had been explained by the
model not seeing the map, which was a hypothesis rather than a finding, and the
honest thing was to test it. So it was given the map: the rendered tree, the
rows the fold would replace marked, and the question asked about the page
rather than the source. It declines 94% of everything, which settles it. The
missing context was not the problem either.

What is left is the plain reading: a model can name a run of calls and cannot
judge whether folding it is a good idea, and no prompt in this family changes
that.

So the shape to build is not a model that judges. It is a model that names and
a structural gate that rejects, which is what `notes.py` already does when it
checks a claimed loop against `n_loops`. Rejecting 7 to prevent 3 is the right
direction under recce's asymmetry: a fold not taken costs rows, a fold taken
wrongly sends the reader somewhere the code does not go.

    ./phase-probe --dry-run sqlmap=~/.cache/recce-corpus/sqlmap/lib
    ssh -N -L 11435:127.0.0.1:11434 dev104 &
    ./phase-probe --calibrate --ollama-host http://127.0.0.1:11435 \
        --model qwen3.6:35b-a3b-coding-mtp-q4_K_M nltk=...
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import ast
import io
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import tokenize
import urllib.error
import urllib.request
import warnings
from dataclasses import dataclass
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Set, Tuple

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))

from recce.extract import discover  # noqa: E402
from recce.graph import resolve  # noqa: E402
from recce.model import EXTERNAL, Func  # noqa: E402
from recce.notes import DEFAULT_HOST, resolve_model  # noqa: E402

# A run has to be this long before folding it saves anything worth a new row.
# Matches `rank._PHASE_MIN_ROWS`; the two are the same judgement.
MIN_RUN = 3

# Lines between two external calls before they stop reading as one run. Calls
# are matched on source line rather than on adjacency in the rendered tree,
# because the tree has already been through `_fit` and its order is not the
# order the statements were written in.
MAX_GAP = 3

# Below this a function is short enough to read whole. Matches
# `extract._PHASE_MIN_LOC`.
MIN_LOC = 30

# Deliberately close to the note prompt's register: one short line, no
# preamble, no restating. The difference that matters is NONE — that answer is
# the whole point of asking, because it is the judgement no structural test
# could make, and a model that never gives it has told you it is not judging.
PROMPT = """\
Below is a run of consecutive calls from inside one Python function, with the
lines around them for context.

Name what this run of calls DOES, as a heading, in under 6 words. Write it the
way a programmer writes a comment above a block: "set up output directories",
"write the json reports", "load and sort the series".

Answer NONE instead if the run is the function's real work rather than
preparation, bookkeeping, logging or cleanup around it. NONE is a good answer
and it is expected often.

No Markdown, no quotes, no trailing full stop, no explanation.

Function `{qualname}` in {module}, lines {start}-{end}:
```python
{source}
```

Heading, or NONE:"""


# The second question, asked only when `--two-stage` is on. Deliberately not
# "is this heading good": a model asked to grade its own answer agrees with
# itself. This is a classification against a stated criterion, and the criterion
# needs the whole function to answer -- which is the same reason `--full`
# exists. The two ideas are one fix.
VERDICT_PROMPT = """\
Inside the Python function below, lines {start}-{end} have been given this
heading, so that those lines would be replaced by it in a summary:

    {label}

Answer KEEP if those lines are one phase of this function among several, and
the heading names that phase.

Answer DROP if those lines are the function's main work, so that replacing them
with the heading would hide what the function actually does.

Answer with one word, KEEP or DROP, and nothing else.

```python
{source}
```

KEEP or DROP:"""


# The third question, asked only when `--map-context` is on. The two before it
# failed for the same reason and it is worth naming: both asked about the code,
# and whether a fold costs the reader anything is not a property of the code.
# It is a property of the page — of what is left standing once the rows go. So
# this one shows the rows recce would actually print, marks the ones the fold
# would replace, and asks about the result rather than about the source.
#
# The failure to watch for is the one the previous two showed: if this declines
# at the same rate on runs an author named as on runs nobody named, the model
# is not judging and no fourth prompt will change that.
MAP_PROMPT = """\
Below is what a code map would print for one Python function. It is a tree of
the calls it makes; a reader uses it to decide what to go and read.

The rows marked >> would be REPLACED by this single line:

    ‹ {label} ›

{tree}

Would a reader of the map still learn what `{qualname}` does, after that
replacement?

Answer KEEP if the heading carries what those rows were saying, so the map is
shorter and no worse.

Answer DROP if the replacement hides something a reader needed — because the
marked rows are what the function is for, or because the heading claims more
than it delivers.

Answer with one word, KEEP or DROP, and nothing else.

KEEP or DROP:"""


def render_tree(node, run_labels: set, depth: int = 0) -> List[str]:
    """The node as a map would print it, with the folded rows marked.

    Deliberately not `render._render_block`: that formats for a terminal, with
    bracket columns and markers, and none of that is what is being asked about.
    What the question needs is which rows stand and which go.

    Rows are matched by label. That is looser than matching by source line and
    can mark a second call of the same name elsewhere in the tree, which for a
    question about what the reader is left with is the safe direction: it
    overstates what the fold takes rather than understating it.
    """
    rows = []
    for child in node.children:
        # Matched on the row's own text. `Node` carried the source line while
        # the fold lived in `rank.py`, and it does not any more, so marking by
        # line number left this mode raising `AttributeError` on every call.
        mark = '>>' if child.label in run_labels else '  '
        bracket = '  [{}]'.format(child.bracket) if child.bracket else ''
        rows.append('{} {}{}{}'.format(mark, '    ' * depth, child.label, bracket))
        rows.extend(render_tree(child, run_labels, depth + 1))
    return rows


def function_tree(func: Func, project, graph, run: List) -> str:
    """The rendered block for one function, with the run's rows marked."""
    from recce import rank

    node = rank._build_tree(func, project, graph, depth_cap=3)
    lines = render_tree(node, {'{}()'.format(c.attr) for c in run})
    head = '   {}({})'.format(func.qualname, ', '.join(func.args[:3]))
    return '\n'.join([head] + lines[:60])


def whole_function(func: Func) -> str:
    """The function's own source, bounded the way `notes._source_of` bounds it."""
    try:
        lines = Path(func.path).read_text(errors='replace').splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return ''
    body = '\n'.join(lines[func.lineno - 1 : func.end_lineno])
    return body[:6000]


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comment-derived phases, which used to live in `recce.extract`.
#
# They were there to fold rows in the map and they no longer do; see the
# rejected table in CLAUDE.md for why. They stay here because the probe needs
# them as ground truth: a run the author put a heading over is the only
# evidence available about which runs deserve one, and `--calibrate` is built
# on it. Keeping them out of the library keeps recce from tokenising every file
# it reads for a feature nothing renders.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


@dataclass
class Phase:
    """A run of statements a comment inside a function body names."""

    label: str
    start: int
    end: int


# A comment that is machinery rather than a heading. These sit above a
# statement exactly where a phase name would and say nothing about what the
# next few lines do.
_PRAGMA_COMMENTS = ('noqa', 'type:', 'pragma:', 'pylint:', 'mypy:', 'fmt:', 'ruff:')


# A note to whoever maintains the code, not a name for what the next lines do.
# Measured across six codebases these are what survives every other filter:
# `XXX not implemented yet`, `Reference: http://bugs.python.org/issue17849`,
# `-- End Removal --`. Each sits exactly where a heading would and names
# nothing.
_ASIDE_COMMENTS = (
    'xxx',
    'todo',
    'fixme',
    'hack',
    'note:',
    'nb:',
    'reference',
    'see http',
    'see:',
    '--',
    'http://',
    'https://',
)


# Below this a function is short enough to read whole, and naming a phase
# inside it is ceremony. The measured shape this defends is the opposite one:
# functions over thirty lines, where 71-92% of old codebases put an interior
# comment and the phases are already named.
_PHASE_MIN_LOC = 30


# How long a comment may be and still read as a heading rather than an
# explanation. A row label longer than this crowds out the tree it sits in.
_PHASE_LABEL_CHARS = 52


def own_line_comments(source: str) -> Dict[int, str]:
    """Comments that sit on a line of their own, by line number.

    A trailing comment annotates the statement it follows; a comment on its own
    line above a run of statements is a heading for them. Only the second kind
    can name a phase, so the distinction is made here and once per file.
    """
    comments: Dict[int, str] = {}
    try:
        # Tokenising re-processes f-string escapes on 3.12+, so a file with a
        # dodgy one warns to stderr with `<string>` for a filename. recce reads
        # other people's code and has no business complaining about it; black's
        # test data put eight such lines into a corpus stats file.
        with warnings.catch_warnings():
            warnings.simplefilter('ignore')
            tokens = list(tokenize.generate_tokens(io.StringIO(source).readline))
        for token in tokens:
            if token.type != tokenize.COMMENT:
                continue
            if not token.line.lstrip().startswith('#'):
                continue  # trailing: it belongs to the statement, not to a run
            text = token.string.lstrip('#').strip()
            if not text or text.startswith(_PRAGMA_COMMENTS):
                continue
            comments[token.start[0]] = text
    except (tokenize.TokenError, IndentationError, SyntaxError):
        return {}
    return comments


def _comment_phases(
    node: ast.AST, comments: Dict[int, str], blanks: Set[int]
) -> List[Phase]:
    """The runs of top-level statements this function's own comments name.

    A phase starts at the first statement under a heading comment and runs to
    the statement before the next heading. Only the body's top level is
    considered: a comment inside a loop is about that iteration, not about a
    stretch of the function.

    Nothing is inferred. Where the author wrote no headings there are no
    phases and the map is exactly as it was.
    """
    body = getattr(node, 'body', [])
    start_line = getattr(node, 'lineno', 0)
    end_line = getattr(node, 'end_lineno', start_line) or start_line
    if end_line - start_line < _PHASE_MIN_LOC or len(body) < 2:
        return []
    heads: List[Tuple[int, str]] = []
    for index, stmt in enumerate(body):
        previous = body[index - 1].end_lineno if index else start_line
        gap = [
            comments[line]
            for line in range((previous or start_line) + 1, stmt.lineno)
            if line in comments
        ]
        # Exactly one line, or it is not a heading. A multi-line comment block
        # is an argument about the code, and picking whichever of its lines
        # happens to be short enough yields a fragment: measured over six
        # codebases the two-or-more case produced labels like "be the way in."
        # and "it because people are relying on it." -- the tail of a sentence
        # whose head is on the line above.
        # A heading is short, and the length is judged here rather than while
        # tokenising so that a long line still counts towards the block. Doing
        # it the other way round dropped the long lines of a paragraph before
        # they were counted, so a three-line argument whose middle line
        # happened to be short passed as a one-line heading.
        if len(gap) != 1 or len(gap[0]) > _PHASE_LABEL_CHARS:
            continue
        lowered = gap[0].lower()
        if lowered.startswith(_ASIDE_COMMENTS) or 'http' in lowered:
            continue
        # A trailing colon introduces something rather than naming it. On the
        # map that raised this, `# Imbio IQ-UIP Findings:` labels the text of a
        # DICOM field and reads as nonsense once it is a row of its own.
        if gap[0].endswith(':'):
            continue
        heads.append((index, gap[0]))
    phases = []
    for position, (index, label) in enumerate(heads):
        stop = heads[position + 1][0] if position + 1 < len(heads) else len(body)
        last = body[_paragraph_end(body, index, stop, blanks)]
        phases.append(
            Phase(
                label=label,
                start=body[index].lineno,
                end=getattr(last, 'end_lineno', last.lineno) or last.lineno,
            )
        )
    return phases


def _paragraph_end(body: List, index: int, stop: int, blanks: Set[int]) -> int:
    """The last statement the heading at `index` actually covers.

    Running to the next heading was wrong and the failure is worth keeping.
    On a real map `# Write derived_from, series_instance_uid to json file` was
    written for two calls and swallowed a third, so `write_json_patient`
    vanished behind a label that does not mention patients. A row hidden
    behind a name that does not cover it is the exact cost the budget ladder
    is arranged to avoid, and here it was self-inflicted.

    A blank line ends a paragraph, and a heading names the paragraph under it.
    That is the convention the author was already following by writing the
    comment where they did, so reading it is of a piece with reading the
    comment itself rather than a new guess.
    """
    last = index
    for position in range(index + 1, stop):
        previous = body[position - 1]
        previous_end = getattr(previous, 'end_lineno', previous.lineno)
        gap = range((previous_end or previous.lineno) + 1, body[position].lineno)
        if any(line in blanks for line in gap):
            break
        last = position
    return last


def runs_of(func: Func) -> List[List]:
    """Runs of external calls close enough together to read as one block."""
    external = sorted(
        (c for c in func.calls if c.kind == EXTERNAL and c.label),
        key=lambda c: c.lineno,
    )
    runs: List[List] = []
    current: List = []

    def worth_keeping(run: List) -> bool:
        # Distinct lines, not distinct calls. `re.findall(re.escape(x))` is
        # three external calls on one line and folding it says nothing: a
        # phase is a run of statements, and a nested expression is one
        # statement however many calls it contains. Two of sqlmap's first
        # three candidates were exactly this.
        return len({c.lineno for c in run}) >= MIN_RUN

    for call in external:
        if current and call.lineno - current[-1].lineno <= MAX_GAP:
            current.append(call)
            continue
        if worth_keeping(current):
            runs.append(current)
        current = [call]
    if worth_keeping(current):
        runs.append(current)
    return runs


_PHASE_CACHE: Dict[str, Dict[Tuple[str, int], List[Phase]]] = {}


def phases_of(func: Func) -> List[Phase]:
    """The comment-named phases of one function, parsed from its own file.

    Keyed on name and end line rather than start line. `Func.lineno` points at
    the first decorator and `ast` points at the `def`, so matching on the start
    silently missed every decorated function -- which shrank the calibration
    set from 25 to 15 on nltk and moved the misses into the unnamed pile, where
    they looked like more work rather than like a bug.

    Cached per file: one parse serves every function in it.
    """
    if func.path not in _PHASE_CACHE:
        table: Dict[Tuple[str, int], List[Phase]] = {}
        try:
            source = Path(func.path).read_text(errors='replace')
            tree = ast.parse(source)
        except (OSError, SyntaxError, ValueError):
            _PHASE_CACHE[func.path] = table
            return []
        comments = own_line_comments(source)
        blanks = {
            number
            for number, text in enumerate(source.splitlines(), start=1)
            if not text.strip()
        }
        for node in ast.walk(tree):
            if isinstance(node, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef)):
                end = getattr(node, 'end_lineno', node.lineno) or node.lineno
                table[(node.name, end)] = _comment_phases(node, comments, blanks)
        _PHASE_CACHE[func.path] = table
    return _PHASE_CACHE[func.path].get((func.name, func.end_lineno), [])


def named_by_author(func: Func, run: List, phases: List[Phase]) -> Optional[str]:
    """The author's own heading for this run, if they wrote one."""
    for phase in phases:
        if phase.start <= run[0].lineno <= phase.end:
            return phase.label
    return None


# Words too common to count as a restatement when a label and a function name
# share them.
_STOPWORDS = frozenset(
    {'the', 'a', 'an', 'and', 'for', 'of', 'to', 'up', 'with', 'in', 'value'}
)


def _words(text: str) -> set:
    """Identifier or phrase, split into comparable words."""
    text = re.sub(r'([a-z0-9])([A-Z])', r'\1 \2', text.split('.')[-1])
    return {
        w for w in re.split(r'[^A-Za-z0-9]+', text.lower()) if w and w not in _STOPWORDS
    }


def restatement(func: Func, label: str) -> float:
    """How much of the function's own name the label gives back.

    A label that restates the function is the fold going wrong: qwen3.6 named
    `parseSqliteTableSchema`'s only run `parse sqlite table schema`, which
    hides the function's substance behind its own name. Measured on its own
    this over-fires -- `build_index` and `set_proxy` score 100% on labels the
    author had also thought worth writing -- so read it beside `coverage`.
    """
    name = _words(func.qualname)
    return len(name & _words(label)) / len(name) if name else 0.0


def coverage(func: Func, run: List) -> float:
    """How much of the function the run spans.

    This is what separates a run that IS the function from a run that is one
    phase of it. A culminating phase can restate the function's name and still
    be worth folding; a run covering most of the body cannot, because folding
    it folds the function.
    """
    span = (func.end_lineno - func.lineno) or 1
    return (run[-1].lineno - run[0].lineno + 1) / span


def excerpt(path: str, start: int, end: int, pad: int = 2) -> str:
    """The run's own lines, with a little context either side."""
    try:
        lines = Path(path).read_text(errors='replace').splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return ''
    lo = max(start - pad - 1, 0)
    hi = min(end + pad, len(lines))
    return '\n'.join(lines[lo:hi])


def ask(
    host: str, model: str, prompt: str, timeout: float, think: bool = False
) -> Optional[str]:
    """One blocking generate call, shaped like `notes._ask`.

    `think` is off by default for the reason `notes._ask` gives: reasoning goes
    into the same `num_predict` budget as the answer, and at 40 tokens it eats
    all of it, so the response comes back empty and every ask reads as a
    failure -- a truncation problem, not a verdict on reasoning.

    `--think` turns it on for the second question only, and the asymmetry is
    measured rather than tidy. Asked to *name* a run, this model does not
    converge: 7385 characters of reasoning at a 2000-token ceiling and 28473 at
    8000, `done_reason` still `length` and the answer still empty both times, so
    raising the ceiling is not a fix. Asked the closed KEEP-or-DROP question it
    stops on its own in three to ten seconds. Naming was never the half that
    needed help.

    Ollama returns the reasoning in a separate `thinking` field on this model,
    so `response` carries the answer alone and nothing downstream changes.
    """
    payload = json.dumps(
        {
            'model': model,
            'prompt': prompt,
            'stream': False,
            'think': think,
            'options': {
                'temperature': 0.0,
                'num_predict': 2000 if think else 40,
            },
        }
    ).encode('utf-8')
    request = urllib.request.Request(
        host.rstrip('/') + '/api/generate',
        data=payload,
        headers={'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
    )
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=timeout) as response:
            body = json.loads(response.read().decode('utf-8'))
    except (urllib.error.URLError, TimeoutError, OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        return 'ERROR: {}'.format(exc)
    raw = (body.get('response') or '').strip()
    return raw.splitlines()[0].strip().strip('"\'`').rstrip('.') if raw else ''


def collect(path: str, calibrate: bool):
    """The runs worth asking about, with the project and graph that found them.

    Both come back because `--map-context` needs them to rebuild the tree the
    question is about.
    """
    project = discover(Path(path).expanduser())
    graph = resolve(project)
    found = []
    for func in project.funcs():
        if (func.end_lineno - func.lineno) < MIN_LOC:
            continue
        phases = phases_of(func)
        for run in runs_of(func):
            author = named_by_author(func, run, phases)
            if calibrate and author is None:
                continue
            if not calibrate and author is not None:
                continue
            found.append((func, run, author))
    return project, graph, found


def main(argv: List[str]) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument('targets', nargs='+', help='name=path, or just a path')
    parser.add_argument(
        '--calibrate',
        action='store_true',
        help='ask about runs the author named, and print both answers',
    )
    parser.add_argument('--model', default=None)
    parser.add_argument('--ollama-host', default=DEFAULT_HOST)
    parser.add_argument('--limit', type=int, default=25, help='runs per target')
    parser.add_argument('--timeout', type=float, default=120.0)
    parser.add_argument(
        '--dry-run',
        action='store_true',
        help='find the runs and print them, without asking anything',
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        '--full',
        action='store_true',
        help="send the whole function rather than the run's own lines",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        '--two-stage',
        action='store_true',
        help='after a heading comes back, ask a second time whether to keep it',
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        '--think',
        action='store_true',
        help='let the model reason first, with a budget big enough to allow it',
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        '--map-context',
        action='store_true',
        help='ask the second question against the rendered tree, not the source',
    )
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    model = None
    if not args.dry_run:
        model = args.model or resolve_model(args.ollama_host)
        if not model:
            print('no model: pass --model, or set RECCE_MODEL', file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        print('model: {} at {}\n'.format(model, args.ollama_host))

    declined = answered = unreadable = 0
    for target in args.targets:
        name, _, path = target.partition('=')
        if not path:
            name, path = os.path.basename(target.rstrip('/')), target
        project, graph, found = collect(path, args.calibrate)
        print(
            '== {} — {} {} runs, showing {}'.format(
                name,
                len(found),
                'named' if args.calibrate else 'unnamed',
                min(len(found), args.limit),
            )
        )
        for func, run, author in found[: args.limit]:
            start, end = run[0].lineno, run[-1].lineno
            calls = ', '.join(dict.fromkeys('{}()'.format(c.attr) for c in run))
            print(
                '\n  {}:{}-{}  {}'.format(
                    os.path.basename(func.path), start, end, func.qualname
                )
            )
            print('    calls: {}'.format(calls[:96]))
            if author:
                print('    author: {}'.format(author))
            if args.dry_run:
                continue
            source = (
                whole_function(func) if args.full else excerpt(func.path, start, end)
            )
            answer = ask(
                args.ollama_host,
                model,
                PROMPT.format(
                    qualname=func.qualname,
                    module=func.module,
                    start=start,
                    end=end,
                    source=source,
                ),
                args.timeout,
            )
            print('    model:  {}'.format(answer or '(empty)'))
            if (
                (args.two_stage or args.map_context)
                and answer
                and not answer.upper().startswith(('NONE', 'ERROR'))
            ):
                if args.map_context:
                    second = MAP_PROMPT.format(
                        label=answer,
                        qualname=func.qualname,
                        tree=function_tree(func, project, graph, run),
                    )
                else:
                    second = VERDICT_PROMPT.format(
                        label=answer,
                        start=start,
                        end=end,
                        source=whole_function(func),
                    )
                verdict = ask(args.ollama_host, model, second, args.timeout, args.think)
                verdict = (verdict or '').strip().upper()
                print('    verdict: {}'.format(verdict or '(empty)'))
                if verdict.startswith('DROP'):
                    answer = 'NONE'
                elif not verdict.startswith('KEEP'):
                    # Neither word came back, usually because the reasoning ran
                    # to the token ceiling. Counting that as KEEP would report a
                    # failed ask as a judgement, which is the trap `notes._ask`
                    # already warns about, so it is counted apart.
                    unreadable += 1
                    answer = None
            if answer and not answer.upper().startswith(('NONE', 'ERROR')):
                print(
                    '    signals: restates name {:.0%}, covers {:.0%} of the function'.format(
                        restatement(func, answer), coverage(func, run)
                    )
                )
            if answer and answer.upper().startswith('NONE'):
                declined += 1
            elif answer and not answer.startswith('ERROR'):
                answered += 1
        print()

    if not args.dry_run:
        total = declined + answered
        print(
            'named {} of {} ({:.0%}), declined {}, unreadable {}'.format(
                answered, total, answered / total if total else 0, declined, unreadable
            )
        )
        print(
            'A model that never declines is generating labels rather than\n'
            'judging, and the fold would bury the substance with the machinery.'
        )
    return 0


if __name__ == '__main__':
    raise SystemExit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
